# Public edge for the remote connector (D-095, D-098, D-100; contract C4).
# Status: example. Mark each directive verified in docs/RESULTS.md when the connector probe passes.
#
# This is a second site, separate from the phone listener in deploy/Caddyfile. It is not a second
# Caddy process: add it to the main Caddy config with a top-level import (docs/MAC-SETUP.md, section 13).
#
# Port 8443 (D-100). The router forwards public TCP 8443 to this Mac's 8443. The phone's mutual-TLS
# site uses the same port. Caddy tells the two apart by host name (SNI), so each client must connect
# by its own host name. Caddy runs as the owner's user, which cannot bind 443 without root.
#
# Why this site has no client certificate: the Claude client cannot present one. Access is
# controlled by the grant bearer token that the phone approves at pairing, by the strict path list
# below (anything else is 404), and by the gateway's per-grant limits. Do not add a client_auth block
# here, and do not add the phone's paths or the phone's host name to this site.
#
# The public host name comes from CONNECTOR_HOST, set in the Caddy LaunchAgent environment. Its DNS
# record must point at this Mac's public address. Do not use `tls internal` here: a private
# certificate is refused by the Claude client.
#
# Certificate (D-100): Let's Encrypt's HTTP-01 and TLS-ALPN-01 challenges need public port 80 or 443,
# which this setup does not forward. The DNS-01 challenge needs no inbound port, so this site uses
# it. Caddy must be built with the DNS provider module (xcaddy build --with <module>). PROVIDER below
# is a placeholder for that module's name. The API token is read from CADDY_DNS_API_TOKEN, which the
# LaunchAgent loads from the owner's secrets file. The token is never written in this repo.
#
# The gateway listens on 127.0.0.1:8789 (connector.port in the config). It is the only upstream.

{$CONNECTOR_HOST}:8443 {
	tls {
		dns PROVIDER {env.CADDY_DNS_API_TOKEN}
	}

	# Exactly seven paths (C4, D-095, owner decision 44). The path-suffixed metadata form is listed
	# because clients look it up first. Matchers are exact: /mcp/ or /mcp?x is not forwarded.
	@connector path /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server /register /authorize /token /mcp

	handle @connector {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8789
	}

	handle {
		respond 404
	}
}
